.TH KITTYPLEX 1 "2026-11-01" "kittyplex 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kittyplex \- terminal multiplexing with Kitty\-owned screens
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kittyplex
.RB [ \-\-heartbeat\-timeout " SECONDS"]
.br
.B kittyplex start
.RB [ \-\-login ]
.RB [ \-\-cwd " DIRECTORY"]
.br
.B kittyplex run0
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B kittyplex ssh
.RB [ \-\-remote\-binary " PATH"]
.RB [ \-\-cwd " DIRECTORY"]
.I DESTINATION
.RB [ \-\- " SSH_OPTIONS..."]
.br
.B kittyplex ctl
.RB [ \-\-socket " PATH"]
.I COMMAND
.br
.B kittyplex doctor
.br
.B kittyplex
.BR \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Kittyplex manages application processes and pseudoterminals while Kitty
retains
an independent screen, scrollback and terminal parser for each session.
It transports application bytes without emulating a terminal.
A group contains up to eight sessions, each displayed in its own native Kitty
tab.
.PP
Install the matching local binary and Kitty integration files before starting.
SSH targets also need kittyplex and xterm-kitty terminfo installed.
The original terminal is the group's carrier and must remain open.
No separate SSH connection per session, forwarding setup or patched Kitty is
needed.
.PP
There is no detach or reattach support.
Connection loss or controller termination closes the group.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS No subcommand
Start the controller in the current environment and directory.
This also works after an ordinary interactive SSH login.
New sessions execute $SHELL with \-i, falling back to /bin/sh \-i.
.TP
.BI \-\-heartbeat\-timeout " SECONDS"
Connection-loss timeout; default 30 seconds, minimum 10.
Heartbeats are sent every five seconds.
This option is supported by the bare controller invocation only.
.SS start
Start a local group using the executing account's shell from the account
database.
.TP
.B \-\-login
Load that account's login environment before starting the controller.
Default to its home directory rather than the caller's current directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-cwd " DIRECTORY"
Set the initial directory.
Without \-\-login, relative directories resolve from the current directory.
With \-\-login, they resolve from the account's home, and the directory is
applied
after login initialization.
In either case, ~ and ~/ resolve to that account's home.
.SS run0
Use run0 to start a group as another local user.
Requires systemd version 258 or newer and normal Polkit authorization.
The target receives its own login environment and account shell.
Kittyplex executes its current binary by absolute path, which must be
accessible
to the target account; Nix store installations provide such a shared path.
.TP
.BR \-u " USER, " \-\-user " USER"
Target user; default root.
.TP
.BR \-g " GROUP, " \-\-group " GROUP"
Target primary group; default the target account's primary group.
.TP
.BR \-D " DIRECTORY, " \-\-chdir " DIRECTORY, " \-\-cwd " DIRECTORY"
Initial directory; default the target's home.
Relative paths resolve from the target's home, after login initialization.
.TP
.B \-\-pty
Request transport PTY allocation before authentication completes.
.TP
.B \-\-pty\-late
Leave authentication on the source terminal until authorization succeeds.
This is the default and cannot be combined with \-\-pty.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ask\-password
Do not prompt for authentication.
This requires authorization already permitted by the host's policy; it grants
no access itself.
.PP
Value options accept separate values, \-\-option=value and attached short
values
such as \-ubob.
Only the options listed here are accepted; this is not a general run0 argument
wrapper.
.SS ssh
Connect using ordinary OpenSSH settings and automatically start the target
controller
with its account's login environment and home directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote\-binary " PATH"
Target executable; default kittyplex on the SSH command PATH.
That PATH may differ from an interactive login PATH.
This names one executable, not a shell command.
.TP
.BI \-\-cwd " DIRECTORY"
Initial directory on the target; default its home.
Relative paths resolve from the target's home, and ~ and ~/ refer to that
home.
.PP
Put connection options after \-\-, following the destination.
For example, use user@server \-\- \-p 2222 \-J jump-host.
The launcher supplies the remote command and forces a foreground PTY.
It rejects \-N, \-f, \-T, \-n, \-O, \-W, \-G and conflicting \-o settings.
No remote command can be supplied through the connection-options list.
Targets must have a compatible kittyplex version (0.4 or newer for automatic
startup).
The launcher does not upload binaries or bootstrap Nix.
.SS ctl
Send one management command to an existing controller on the same host and
account.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
Controller socket; defaults to KITTYPLEX_SOCKET in managed shells.
This is the target controller's socket, not the local display broker's socket.
.TP
.B new
Create and select a shell in a new tab.
Acknowledgement is asynchronous: the new session may not yet appear in status.
.TP
.BI split " right|below"
Create a session to the right of or below the currently selected session.
Creation is asynchronous and uses the same account and transport.
.TP
.BR next , " previous"
Select the next or previous session in creation order, wrapping around.
.TP
.BI select " N"
Select a session by its current one-based position.
Positions shift as sessions exit; reordering Kitty tabs does not change them.
.TP
.B status
Print the selected position, ready-session count, session IDs and shell PIDs.
.TP
.B quit
Close the entire group.
.SS doctor
Probe the local scoped Kitty callback without creating a group or changing
user.
Report the integration version, desktop API, carrier version, selected local
binary,
run0 version and SSH availability.
Requires a real configured Kitty terminal, and can run in a managed view.
Missing optional tools are reported without making an otherwise successful
Kitty probe fail.
The command does not verify remote installations or attempt authentication.
.SH HELP AND VERSION
Every public subcommand accepts \-h or \-\-help without requiring a terminal,
controller socket, remote connection or authentication.
The top-level \-V and \-\-version options print the program version.
.SH KEYBOARD CONTROLS
Press Ctrl-Shift-M, release it, then press an action key:
.TP
.BR t , " c"
Create and select a shell in a new tab.
.TP
.B v
Vertical split: create a session on the right.
.TP
.B h
Horizontal split: create a session below.
.TP
.B w
Close the focused pane and its session.
.TP
.B ?
Show local help over the focused pane.
Escape dismisses help without reaching the application; Up/Down and
Page Up/Page Down scroll it.
.TP
.BR n , " p"
Select the next or previous session.
.TP
.B 1\(en9
Select a session at that current position, if present.
.TP
.B q
Close the group.
.TP
.B Escape
Cancel management mode.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Shift-M
Send one literal prefix chord to the application.
.PP
Management shortcuts apply only in managed views and end after one action.
Ordinary Enter/Ctrl-M remains application input.
Kitty's native pane/tab selection, resizing, movement and closure also work.
Closing a tab closes all of its sessions.
Repeated splits can create nested arrangements.
Help does not create a session or pause application output.
.SH KITTY ACTIONS
The supplied configuration defines kittyplex-local, kittyplex-run0 and
kittyplex-ssh
as trusted local Kitty actions, not shell commands.
Bind them to shortcuts in kitty.conf if desired; no additional global
shortcuts are installed.
Actions create independent groups, including when invoked from an existing
managed view.
Direct CLI nesting is rejected.
.PP
For example, a Kitty mapping can contain:
.PP
.nf
map ctrl+alt+f8 kittyplex\-run0 \-u bob \-\-pty
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KITTYPLEX_BINARY
Absolute local binary override read by the Kitty integration.
Set it before starting Kitty.
Otherwise packaged helpers use their matching Nix store binary, falling back
to PATH
when running from a source installation.
A remote environment variable cannot select the local binary.
.TP
.B KITTYPLEX_SOCKET
Private controller socket, supplied to managed shells and used by ctl.
Its presence also prevents nested CLI launches.
.TP
.B KITTYPLEX_SESSION
Stable session ID supplied to a managed shell; distinct from its current
position.
.TP
.B SHELL
Interactive shell for a bare controller invocation, falling back to /bin/sh.
The start, run0 and ssh bootstraps instead select the executing account's
shell.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Preferred directory for the controller's private socket directory when usable;
otherwise /tmp is used.
.SH FILES
In Kitty's configuration directory (normally ~/.config/kitty), install
kittyplex.py,
kittyplex_auth.py, kittyplex_watcher.py, kittyplex_launch.py,
kittyplex_help.py and kittyplex.conf.
Add include kittyplex.conf to the existing kitty.conf.
The configuration enables password-mode remote control with a scoped callback.
Use Kitty's actual configuration directory when it has been customized.
.PP
Nix packages provide the files under share/kittyplex/kitty and this manual
under
share/man/man1.
Kitty configuration and source-build installation instructions are maintained
at:
.PP
https://github.com/claes/kittyplex/blob/master/docs/integration-instructions.md
.SH LIFECYCLE AND LIMITS
New shells inherit the controller's initial directory and environment, rather
than
the most recently used shell's current directory.
Login profiles must allow noninteractive commands to complete.
Tabs for bootstrap-created groups retain a user@host identity prefix while
following application title changes; titles are not an authentication proof.
.PP
Exiting a shell or closing its session tab ends that session.
Exiting the last shell ends the group.
Quitting closes owned views and restores the carrier's terminal settings.
CLI launches return to their source shell.
Kitty action launches close the carrier on success and retain errors until
Enter is pressed.
Unrelated Kitty windows and tabs are preserved.
.PP
Closing the carrier, losing transport, or losing the controller ends the
group.
Cleanup hangs up shells and foreground process groups; jobs deliberately
ignoring
hangup can survive.
Bounded queues apply backpressure rather than discarding hidden-session
output.
The original-terminal carrier adds framing overhead and is experimental.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Local commands return zero on success and one on error.
The run0 and ssh launchers replace themselves with their transport and
therefore
return its exit status, including target-command failure status.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
kittyplex doctor
kittyplex start
kittyplex run0 \-u bob \-\-pty
kittyplex run0 \-u bob \-D /srv/work
kittyplex ssh user@server \-\- \-p 2222
kittyplex ssh \-\-remote\-binary=/opt/kittyplex/bin/kittyplex server
kittyplex ctl status
kittyplex ctl \-\-socket /path/to/control.sock status
kittyplex \-\-heartbeat\-timeout 60
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kitty (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR run0 (1)
